/* Verse Games: THE WHOLE COUNSEL · 1 Samuel chapters 11-15
   Verse text is filled/verified from the KJV by tools/fill-verses.js */
VG.registerBibleChapters("1samuel", [
{n:11, name:"Saul Rescues Jabesh-Gilead", seal:{ref:"1 Samuel 11:13",verse:"And Saul said, There shall not a man be put to death this day: for to day the LORD hath wrought salvation in Israel."}, facts:["Did you know Nahash demanded the right eyes of Jabesh-gilead as a living insult to all Israel, turning one town's suffering into a national reproach (1 Samuel 11:2)?","Did you know Saul's fury at the news was not mere human anger? The Spirit of God came upon him when he heard the tidings, and his anger was kindled greatly (1 Samuel 11:6).","Did you know the Ammonites were so scattered in the morning watch that 'there were not left two of them together' (1 Samuel 11:11)?"], questions:[
{q:"Who besieged Jabesh-gilead at the start of the chapter?", options:["Nahash the Ammonite","Achish of Gath","Hanun king of Ammon","Benhadad of Syria"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 11:1",verse:"Then Nahash the Ammonite came up, and encamped against Jabesh-gilead: and all the men of Jabesh said unto Nahash, Make a covenant with us, and we will serve thee.", insight:"Trouble came from an old enemy; God often lets the pressure rise so that His deliverance is unmistakable."},
{q:"What covenant did the men of Jabesh propose to Nahash?", options:["Make a covenant with us, and we will serve thee","Give us thirty days to gather an army","Take our gold and leave us alone","Let us fight one champion for the city"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 11:1",verse:"Then Nahash the Ammonite came up, and encamped against Jabesh-gilead: and all the men of Jabesh said unto Nahash, Make a covenant with us, and we will serve thee.", insight:"They bargained with an enemy who had no mercy; compromise with cruelty is never safety."},
{q:"What cruel condition did Nahash set for the covenant?", options:["That he thrust out all their right eyes","That they pay a yearly tribute of silver","That they surrender their swords and spears","That they give him their firstborn sons"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 11:2",verse:"And Nahash the Ammonite answered them, On this condition will I make a covenant with you, that I may thrust out all your right eyes, and lay it for a reproach upon all Israel.", insight:"Nahash wanted to brand Israel with shame; God's people were never meant to carry the enemy's mark."},
{q:"How many days of respite did the elders of Jabesh ask for?", options:["Seven days","Three days","Forty days","One month"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 11:3",verse:"And the elders of Jabesh said unto him, Give us seven days respite, that we may send messengers unto all the coasts of Israel: and then, if there be no man to save us, we will come out to thee.", insight:"Seven days of hope; even a small window of time can be an act of faith."},
{q:"Where did the messengers come, and how did the people react to the news?", options:["To Gibeah of Saul, and the people wept aloud","To Ramah, and the people armed for war","To Gilgal, and the people sent for Samuel","To Bethel, and the people hid in fear"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 11:4",verse:"Then came the messengers to Gibeah of Saul, and told the tidings in the ears of the people: and all the people lifted up their voices, and wept.", insight:"The whole town wept; shared grief is often the beginning of shared courage."},
{q:"What was Saul doing when the tidings reached him?", options:["Coming after the herd out of the field","Offering sacrifice at Gilgal","Sitting under a pomegranate tree","Numbering the army at Bezek"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 11:5",verse:"And, behold, Saul came after the herd out of the field; and Saul said, What aileth the people that they weep? And they told him the tidings of the men of Jabesh.", insight:"He came in from the field like any farmer; God can summon a king from ordinary work."},
{q:"What came upon Saul when he heard the news?", options:["The Spirit of God, and his anger was kindled greatly","A spirit of fear, and he hid himself","A deep sleep that lasted till morning","A vision of the Philistine army"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 11:6",verse:"And the Spirit of God came upon Saul when he heard those tidings, and his anger was kindled greatly.", insight:"Holy anger burned in him; the Spirit can kindle a righteous fire for the oppressed."},
{q:"What did Saul do with the yoke of oxen?", options:["He hewed them in pieces and sent the pieces through all Israel","He yoked them and plowed the field","He offered them as a burnt offering","He sold them to buy weapons"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 11:7",verse:"And he took a yoke of oxen, and hewed them in pieces, and sent them throughout all the coasts of Israel by the hands of messengers, saying, Whosoever cometh not forth after Saul and after Samuel, so shall it be done unto his oxen. And the fear of the LORD fell on the people, and they came out with one consent.", insight:"He broke his own oxen to break Israel's apathy; sometimes urgency must be shown, not only spoken."},
{q:"How many men answered Saul's summons at Bezek?", options:["Three hundred thousand of Israel and thirty thousand of Judah","One hundred thousand of Israel and ten thousand of Judah","Six hundred men of Benjamin","Three thousand chosen men"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 11:8",verse:"And when he numbered them in Bezek, the children of Israel were three hundred thousand, and the men of Judah thirty thousand.", insight:"Three hundred and thirty thousand came out as one; the fear of the LORD can unite a divided people."},
{q:"What promise did Saul send to the men of Jabesh?", options:["Tomorrow, by the time the sun is hot, you shall have help","In seven days we will break the siege","Samuel will pray and the enemy will flee","Bring your gold and we will ransom you"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 11:9",verse:"And they said unto the messengers that came, Thus shall ye say unto the men of Jabesh-gilead, To morrow, by that time the sun be hot, ye shall have help. And the messengers came and shewed it to the men of Jabesh; and they were glad.", insight:"Help by the heat of the day; God's rescue often has an appointed hour."},
{q:"What message did the men of Jabesh send back to Nahash?", options:["Tomorrow we will come out to you; do with us as seems good to you","We refuse your covenant forever","Our king Saul will pay your price in silver","We have fled the city in the night"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 11:10",verse:"Therefore the men of Jabesh said, To morrow we will come out unto you, and ye shall do with us all that seemeth good unto you.", insight:"They spoke calmly to their tormentor; faith can face the enemy without flinching."},
{q:"Into how many companies did Saul divide his army?", options:["Three","Two","Seven","Twelve"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 11:11",verse:"And it was so on the morrow, that Saul put the people in three companies; and they came into the midst of the host in the morning watch, and slew the Ammonites until the heat of the day: and it came to pass, that they which remained were scattered, so that two of them were not left together.", insight:"Three companies, one purpose; wise plans still need a united heart."},
{q:"When did Saul strike the Ammonites?", options:["In the morning watch, and slew them until the heat of the day","At midnight, by torchlight","At sunset, as the siege began","At noon, in the open field"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 11:11",verse:"And it was so on the morrow, that Saul put the people in three companies; and they came into the midst of the host in the morning watch, and slew the Ammonites until the heat of the day: and it came to pass, that they which remained were scattered, so that two of them were not left together.", insight:"The morning watch; God loves to turn the darkest hour before dawn into victory."},
{q:"How did the men of Jabesh react when the messengers brought Saul's promise?", options:["They were glad","They wept in despair","They fled the city","They mocked the messengers"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 11:9",verse:"And they said unto the messengers that came, Thus shall ye say unto the men of Jabesh-gilead, To morrow, by that time the sun be hot, ye shall have help. And the messengers came and shewed it to the men of Jabesh; and they were glad.", insight:"Gladness before the victory; faith rejoices at the promise, not only at the proof."},
{q:"What did the people want done to those who had questioned Saul's kingship?", options:["Bring them out that we may put them to death","Drive them out of Israel forever","Make them serve as slaves","Forgive them and forget the matter"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 11:12",verse:"And the people said unto Samuel, Who is he that said, Shall Saul reign over us? bring the men, that we may put them to death.", insight:"Victory can make men cruel; Saul's answer would show whether triumph had taught him mercy."},
{q:"What did Saul say about putting men to death that day?", options:["There shall not a man be put to death this day, for the LORD has wrought salvation","Let every traitor die before sunset","The guilty shall be stoned at Gilgal","Justice must wait until the war is over"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 11:13",verse:"And Saul said, There shall not a man be put to death this day: for to day the LORD hath wrought salvation in Israel.", insight:"He gave God the credit and spared his critics; mercy after victory marks a king after God's heart."},
{q:"Where did Samuel tell the people to go to renew the kingdom?", options:["To Gilgal","To Shiloh","To Bethel","To Mizpeh"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 11:14",verse:"Then said Samuel to the people, Come, and let us go to Gilgal, and renew the kingdom there.", insight:"Gilgal, where Israel first camped in the land; renewal often means returning to where faith began."},
{q:"What did all the people do at Gilgal?", options:["Made Saul king before the LORD and sacrificed peace offerings","Built a temple for the ark","Crowned Samuel as judge forever","Burned the weapons of the Ammonites"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 11:15",verse:"And all the people went to Gilgal; and there they made Saul king before the LORD in Gilgal; and there they sacrificed sacrifices of peace offerings before the LORD; and there Saul and all the men of Israel rejoiced greatly.", insight:"They made him king before the LORD; every true throne is set up in God's presence."},
{q:"Why did the men of Gibeah weep when the messengers spoke?", options:["Because of the cruel tidings about Jabesh-gilead","Because Saul had threatened them","Because the harvest had failed","Because Samuel had rebuked them"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 11:4",verse:"Then came the messengers to Gibeah of Saul, and told the tidings in the ears of the people: and all the people lifted up their voices, and wept.", insight:"They wept for strangers in danger; compassion for distant brethren is the seed of courage."},
{q:"How did Israel come out at Saul's call?", options:["With one consent, as the fear of the LORD fell on them","Slowly, over many days","Only the tribe of Judah answered","In anger, ready to fight Saul"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 11:7",verse:"And he took a yoke of oxen, and hewed them in pieces, and sent them throughout all the coasts of Israel by the hands of messengers, saying, Whosoever cometh not forth after Saul and after Samuel, so shall it be done unto his oxen. And the fear of the LORD fell on the people, and they came out with one consent.", insight:"One consent, one fear; when God moves a people, He moves them together."}
]},
{n:12, name:"Samuel's Farewell", seal:{ref:"1 Samuel 12:24",verse:"Only fear the LORD, and serve him in truth with all your heart: for consider how great things he hath done for you."}, facts:["Did you know Samuel asked for thunder and rain during the wheat harvest, the dry season in Israel, so the miracle would be unmistakable as God's sign (1 Samuel 12:17)?","Did you know the people themselves confessed they had 'added unto all our sins this evil, to ask us a king' (1 Samuel 12:19)?","Did you know Samuel counted it a sin to stop praying for the people: 'God forbid that I should sin against the LORD in ceasing to pray for you' (1 Samuel 12:23)?"], questions:[
{q:"What did Samuel say he had done about the people's request?", options:["Hearkened to their voice and made a king over them","Refused them and kept the judgeship","Sent them to ask the LORD themselves","Anointed David instead of Saul"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 12:1",verse:"And Samuel said unto all Israel, Behold, I have hearkened unto your voice in all that ye said unto me, and have made a king over you.", insight:"Samuel had listened, though God had warned them; even our mistaken prayers are sometimes granted, to teach us."},
{q:"How did Samuel describe his own life before the people?", options:["Old and grayheaded, having walked before them since childhood","Young and strong, ready for battle","A prophet only, never a judge","A stranger among them"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 12:2",verse:"And now, behold, the king walketh before you: and I am old and grayheaded; and, behold, my sons are with you: and I have walked before you from my childhood unto this day.", insight:"A lifetime of integrity gave his words weight; character is the loudest part of any farewell."},
{q:"What did Samuel ask the people to witness against him?", options:["Whose ox or ass he had taken, whom he had defrauded or oppressed","How many battles he had won","How much gold he had gathered","Which cities he had built"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 12:3",verse:"Behold, here I am: witness against me before the LORD, and before his anointed: whose ox have I taken? or whose ass have I taken? or whom have I defrauded? whom have I oppressed? or of whose hand have I received any bribe to blind mine eyes therewith? and I will restore it you.", insight:"He invited examination; a clear conscience can stand in the open."},
{q:"What was the people's verdict on Samuel?", options:["Thou hast not defrauded nor oppressed us","Thou hast been harsh and unjust","We cannot remember thy deeds","Thou hast favored thy sons"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 12:4",verse:"And they said, Thou hast not defrauded us, nor oppressed us, neither hast thou taken ought of any man’s hand.", insight:"They cleared him unanimously; it is a rare gift to finish a life's work with everyone as witness to your honesty."},
{q:"Who did Samuel call as witness that day?", options:["The LORD and His anointed","The elders of Israel alone","The ark and the tabernacle","The stones of Gilgal"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 12:5",verse:"And he said unto them, The LORD is witness against you, and his anointed is witness this day, that ye have not found ought in my hand. And they answered, He is witness.", insight:"He appealed to heaven, not to popularity; the highest court is the one that matters."},
{q:"Whom did Samuel say the LORD sent to bring Israel out of Egypt?", options:["Moses and Aaron","Joshua and Caleb","Gideon and Barak","Eli and Samuel"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 12:8",verse:"When Jacob was come into Egypt, and your fathers cried unto the LORD, then the LORD sent Moses and Aaron, which brought forth your fathers out of Egypt, and made them dwell in this place.", insight:"Moses and Aaron, sent by God; deliverance has always been His initiative, not ours."},
{q:"Where did Israel's fathers dwell before God brought them into the land?", options:["In Kadesh","In Goshen","In Haran","In Shiloh"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 12:8",verse:"When Jacob was come into Egypt, and your fathers cried unto the LORD, then the LORD sent Moses and Aaron, which brought forth your fathers out of Egypt, and made them dwell in this place.", insight:"Kadesh, the waiting place; God often keeps His people in the wilderness before the promise."},
{q:"Why did the LORD sell Israel into the hands of their enemies?", options:["Because they forgot the LORD their God","Because they were too few to fight","Because their kings were weak","Because the ark had been captured"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 12:9",verse:"And when they forgat the LORD their God, he sold them into the hand of Sisera, captain of the host of Hazor, and into the hand of the Philistines, and into the hand of the king of Moab, and they fought against them.", insight:"Forgetting God is the root of bondage; memory of His mercies is the root of freedom."},
{q:"Which enemies did Samuel name as Israel's oppressors?", options:["Sisera, the Philistines, and the king of Moab","Egypt, Edom, and Babylon","Amalek, Midian, and Hazor","The Canaanites, Hittites, and Jebusites"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 12:9",verse:"And when they forgat the LORD their God, he sold them into the hand of Sisera, captain of the host of Hazor, and into the hand of the Philistines, and into the hand of the king of Moab, and they fought against them.", insight:"Every generation's oppressor is named; God keeps a record, and so should we."},
{q:"What did Israel confess when they cried to the LORD?", options:["We have sinned, forsaken the LORD, and served Baalim and Ashtaroth","We have been faithful and deserve deliverance","Our judges have failed us","The nations are too strong for us"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 12:10",verse:"And they cried unto the LORD, and said, We have sinned, because we have forsaken the LORD, and have served Baalim and Ashtaroth: but now deliver us out of the hand of our enemies, and we will serve thee.", insight:"Honest confession names the sin; vague regret never sets anyone free."},
{q:"Which deliverers did Samuel list?", options:["Jerubbaal, Bedan, Jephthah, and Samuel","Othniel, Ehud, and Shamgar","Moses, Joshua, and Caleb","David, Solomon, and Hezekiah"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 12:11",verse:"And the LORD sent Jerubbaal, and Bedan, and Jephthah, and Samuel, and delivered you out of the hand of your enemies on every side, and ye dwelled safe.", insight:"God raised rescuer after rescuer; His faithfulness outlasts our forgetfulness."},
{q:"What threat had driven Israel to demand a king?", options:["Nahash the king of Ammon coming against them","The Philistines seizing the ark","A famine in the land","Civil war among the tribes"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 12:12",verse:"And when ye saw that Nahash the king of the children of Ammon came against you, ye said unto me, Nay; but a king shall reign over us: when the LORD your God was your king.", insight:"Fear of Nahash made them trade their King; panic is a poor counselor."},
{q:"Who, Samuel said, was truly Israel's king all along?", options:["The LORD their God","Samuel himself","The elders of Israel","No one; they were free"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 12:12",verse:"And when ye saw that Nahash the king of the children of Ammon came against you, ye said unto me, Nay; but a king shall reign over us: when the LORD your God was your king.", insight:"The LORD was their king; asking for another was not an upgrade but a demotion."},
{q:"What did Samuel say would keep both people and king safe?", options:["Fearing the LORD, serving Him, and obeying His voice","Building strong walls and armies","Making treaties with the nations","Offering many sacrifices"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 12:14",verse:"If ye will fear the LORD, and serve him, and obey his voice, and not rebel against the commandment of the LORD, then shall both ye and also the king that reigneth over you continue following the LORD your God:", insight:"One condition, plain as day: fear Him, serve Him, obey Him; blessing has always had the same address."},
{q:"What miraculous sign did Samuel call for?", options:["Thunder and rain during the wheat harvest","Fire from heaven on the altar","The sun standing still","An earthquake at Gilgal"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 12:17",verse:"Is it not wheat harvest to day? I will call unto the LORD, and he shall send thunder and rain; that ye may perceive and see that your wickedness is great, which ye have done in the sight of the LORD, in asking you a king.", insight:"Rain in harvest, the dry season; God signed His sermon with weather, so none could miss it."},
{q:"How did the people respond when the thunder and rain came?", options:["They greatly feared the LORD and Samuel","They fled to their tents in anger","They laughed at the old prophet","They crowned Saul at once"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 12:18",verse:"So Samuel called unto the LORD; and the LORD sent thunder and rain that day: and all the people greatly feared the LORD and Samuel.", insight:"Fear fell on them; sometimes God must shake the sky to reach the heart."},
{q:"What did the people ask Samuel to do for them?", options:["Pray for his servants to the LORD, that they die not","Give them back their old judges","Curse Nahash the Ammonite","Build an altar at Gilgal"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 12:19",verse:"And all the people said unto Samuel, Pray for thy servants unto the LORD thy God, that we die not: for we have added unto all our sins this evil, to ask us a king.", insight:"They asked for prayer; the first right move after conviction is to ask someone godly to pray."},
{q:"What evil did the people say they had added to all their sins?", options:["To ask for a king","To forget the exodus","To serve Baalim","To reject Samuel's sons"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 12:19",verse:"And all the people said unto Samuel, Pray for thy servants unto the LORD thy God, that we die not: for we have added unto all our sins this evil, to ask us a king.", insight:"They named it plainly; naming the sin is the doorway to grace."},
{q:"What did Samuel say it would be a sin for him to do?", options:["To cease praying for the people","To anoint another king","To leave Gilgal","To speak harshly to Saul"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 12:23",verse:"Moreover as for me, God forbid that I should sin against the LORD in ceasing to pray for you: but I will teach you the good and the right way:", insight:"Ceasing to pray, he called sin; intercession is not optional kindness, it is duty."},
{q:"What was Samuel's closing warning to Israel?", options:["If you do wickedly, you shall be consumed, both you and your king","The Philistines will return within a year","Nahash will besiege you again","Samuel would never pray for them again"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 12:25",verse:"But if ye shall still do wickedly, ye shall be consumed, both ye and your king.", insight:"The warning stands to the end; grace never cancels the consequences of a wicked turn."}
]},
{n:13, name:"Saul's Foolish Sacrifice", seal:{ref:"1 Samuel 13:14",verse:"But now thy kingdom shall not continue: the LORD hath sought him a man after his own heart, and the LORD hath commanded him to be captain over his people, because thou hast not kept that which the LORD commanded thee."}, facts:["Did you know the Philistines gathered thirty thousand chariots and six thousand horsemen against Israel, 'as the sand which is on the sea shore in multitude' (1 Samuel 13:5)?","Did you know the Philistines banned blacksmiths in Israel so the Hebrews could not make swords or spears, forcing them to pay Philistines to sharpen their farm tools (1 Samuel 13:19-20)?","Did you know Samuel's verdict, 'Thou hast done foolishly,' cost Saul's house the kingdom, though the LORD would have established it 'for ever' if he had obeyed (1 Samuel 13:13-14)?"], questions:[
{q:"How many men did Saul choose for his standing force?", options:["Three thousand","Thirty thousand","Six hundred","Two hundred thousand"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 13:2",verse:"Saul chose him three thousand men of Israel; whereof two thousand were with Saul in Michmash and in mount Bethel, and a thousand were with Jonathan in Gibeah of Benjamin: and the rest of the people he sent every man to his tent.", insight:"Three thousand chosen; small beginnings, but God had done more with less."},
{q:"Where were the two thousand who stayed with Saul?", options:["In Michmash and in Mount Bethel","In Gibeah of Benjamin","At Gilgal by the Jordan","In the valley of Elah"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 13:2",verse:"Saul chose him three thousand men of Israel; whereof two thousand were with Saul in Michmash and in mount Bethel, and a thousand were with Jonathan in Gibeah of Benjamin: and the rest of the people he sent every man to his tent.", insight:"Michmash, a mountain pass; the battle would be fought where the army was posted."},
{q:"Who abode with Saul at Gibeah of Benjamin?", options:["Jonathan his son, and the people with them","Samuel and the elders","The Kenites","Abner and his captains"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 13:16",verse:"And Saul, and Jonathan his son, and the people that were present with them, abode in Gibeah of Benjamin: but the Philistines encamped in Michmash.", insight:"Father and son together at Gibeah; soon their paths in faith would part."},
{q:"What bold strike did Jonathan make?", options:["He smote the garrison of the Philistines in Geba","He burned the Philistine chariots","He captured the king of the Philistines","He destroyed the idols of Ashdod"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 13:3",verse:"And Jonathan smote the garrison of the Philistines that was in Geba, and the Philistines heard of it. And Saul blew the trumpet throughout all the land, saying, Let the Hebrews hear.", insight:"Jonathan struck first; courage in the young can shame the caution of the old."},
{q:"What did Saul do after Jonathan's victory?", options:["Blew the trumpet through all the land, saying, Let the Hebrews hear","Offered a peace offering at Gilgal","Sent messengers to make a treaty","Hid the army in the caves"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 13:3",verse:"And Jonathan smote the garrison of the Philistines that was in Geba, and the Philistines heard of it. And Saul blew the trumpet throughout all the land, saying, Let the Hebrews hear.", insight:"He sounded the trumpet; good news deserves a loud witness."},
{q:"How many chariots did the Philistines gather at Michmash?", options:["Thirty thousand","Three thousand","Six hundred","One thousand"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 13:5",verse:"And the Philistines gathered themselves together to fight with Israel, thirty thousand chariots, and six thousand horsemen, and people as the sand which is on the sea shore in multitude: and they came up, and pitched in Michmash, eastward from Beth-aven.", insight:"Thirty thousand chariots; the odds were meant to teach Israel to trust, not to count."},
{q:"How many horsemen came with them?", options:["Six thousand","Sixty thousand","Six hundred","Twelve thousand"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 13:5",verse:"And the Philistines gathered themselves together to fight with Israel, thirty thousand chariots, and six thousand horsemen, and people as the sand which is on the sea shore in multitude: and they came up, and pitched in Michmash, eastward from Beth-aven.", insight:"Six thousand horsemen, and people as the sand; faith does not need the numbers to be fair."},
{q:"Where did the frightened Israelites hide?", options:["In caves, thickets, rocks, high places, and pits","Behind the walls of Jerusalem","In the cities of Judah","Across the Jordan in Moab"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 13:6",verse:"When the men of Israel saw that they were in a strait, (for the people were distressed,) then the people did hide themselves in caves, and in thickets, and in rocks, and in high places, and in pits.", insight:"They hid in holes; fear makes even free men live like fugitives."},
{q:"Where did some of the Hebrews flee?", options:["Over Jordan to the land of Gad and Gilead","To Egypt for safety","To the Philistine camp","To the wilderness of Zin"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 13:7",verse:"And some of the Hebrews went over Jordan to the land of Gad and Gilead. As for Saul, he was yet in Gilgal, and all the people followed him trembling.", insight:"They ran from the battle God meant them to win; flight is sometimes just fear in motion."},
{q:"How long did Saul wait at Gilgal for Samuel?", options:["Seven days, according to the appointed time","Three days","Forty days","One whole month"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 13:8",verse:"And he tarried seven days, according to the set time that Samuel had appointed: but Samuel came not to Gilgal; and the people were scattered from him.", insight:"Seven days, the set time; obedience often means waiting the full term, not almost."},
{q:"What did Saul offer when Samuel had not yet come?", options:["A burnt offering and peace offerings","A sin offering of doves","The firstfruits of the harvest","A sacrifice of thanksgiving"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 13:9",verse:"And Saul said, Bring hither a burnt offering to me, and peace offerings. And he offered the burnt offering.", insight:"He offered without orders; zeal without obedience is only noise."},
{q:"What three reasons did Saul give Samuel for his haste?", options:["The people scattered, Samuel came not in time, and the Philistines gathered at Michmash","The altar was ready, the priests were waiting, and the sun was setting","The Kenites had warned him, the spies had returned, and the moon was full","The oxen were yoked, the trumpets were blown, and the enemy was near"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 13:11",verse:"And Samuel said, What hast thou done? And Saul said, Because I saw that the people were scattered from me, and that thou camest not within the days appointed, and that the Philistines gathered themselves together at Michmash;", insight:"Three excuses, all about pressure; pressure reveals what we trust."},
{q:"Why did Saul say he forced himself to offer?", options:["Because he had not made supplication to the LORD","Because the people demanded a sign","Because the Philistines mocked his God","Because Samuel had commanded it"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 13:12",verse:"Therefore said I, The Philistines will come down now upon me to Gilgal, and I have not made supplication unto the LORD: I forced myself therefore, and offered a burnt offering.", insight:"He forced himself, he said; disobedience dressed as devotion still disobeys."},
{q:"How did Samuel judge what Saul had done?", options:["Thou hast done foolishly","Thou hast done wisely","Thou hast done well","Thou hast done nothing wrong"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 13:13",verse:"And Samuel said to Saul, Thou hast done foolishly: thou hast not kept the commandment of the LORD thy God, which he commanded thee: for now would the LORD have established thy kingdom upon Israel for ever.", insight:"Foolishly, plain and short; God's verdicts need no decoration."},
{q:"What would the LORD have done if Saul had obeyed?", options:["Established his kingdom upon Israel for ever","Made him priest as well as king","Given him the Philistine lands","Doubled his army overnight"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 13:13",verse:"And Samuel said to Saul, Thou hast done foolishly: thou hast not kept the commandment of the LORD thy God, which he commanded thee: for now would the LORD have established thy kingdom upon Israel for ever.", insight:"A kingdom forever, lost in a moment; obedience is the shortest road to permanence."},
{q:"What kind of man had the LORD sought to replace Saul?", options:["A man after His own heart","A mighty man of war","A wealthy man of Benjamin","An elder of the people"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 13:14",verse:"But now thy kingdom shall not continue: the LORD hath sought him a man after his own heart, and the LORD hath commanded him to be captain over his people, because thou hast not kept that which the LORD commanded thee.", insight:"A man after His own heart; God looks past the crown to the heart beneath it."},
{q:"How many men were left with Saul when Samuel departed?", options:["About six hundred","About three thousand","About thirty thousand","About sixty"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 13:15",verse:"And Samuel arose, and gat him up from Gilgal unto Gibeah of Benjamin. And Saul numbered the people that were present with him, about six hundred men.", insight:"Six hundred trembling men; disobedience shrinks an army faster than any enemy."},
{q:"How many companies of Philistine spoilers went out, and where did they turn?", options:["Three: toward Ophrah, Beth-horon, and Zeboim","Two: toward Gilgal and Gibeah","Seven: toward every city of Judah","One: straight toward Saul's camp"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 13:17",verse:"And the spoilers came out of the camp of the Philistines in three companies: one company turned unto the way that leadeth to Ophrah, unto the land of Shual:", insight:"Three roads of raiders; the enemy moves boldly when God's people move fearfully."},
{q:"Why was there no smith in all the land of Israel?", options:["Lest the Hebrews make swords or spears","Because they had all fled to Egypt","Because Saul had dismissed them","Because iron was unknown in Israel"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 13:19",verse:"Now there was no smith found throughout all the land of Israel: for the Philistines said, Lest the Hebrews make them swords or spears:", insight:"No smith, no swords; oppression often begins by disarming the oppressed."},
{q:"On the day of battle, who alone had sword or spear?", options:["Saul and Jonathan his son","Saul and Abner","Jonathan and his armor-bearer","The six hundred men"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 13:22",verse:"So it came to pass in the day of battle, that there was neither sword nor spear found in the hand of any of the people that were with Saul and Jonathan: but with Saul and with Jonathan his son was there found.", insight:"Two swords against an army; God was setting the stage for Jonathan's faith."}
]},
{n:14, name:"Jonathan's Bold Attack", seal:{ref:"1 Samuel 14:6",verse:"And Jonathan said to the young man that bare his armour, Come, and let us go over unto the garrison of these uncircumcised: it may be that the LORD will work for us: for there is no restraint to the LORD to save by many or by few."}, facts:["Did you know Jonathan's famous line, 'there is no restraint to the LORD to save by many or by few,' was spoken before only two men attacked a whole garrison (1 Samuel 14:6)?","Did you know Jonathan tasted honey from the end of his rod without ever hearing his father's oath, and later said simply, 'I did but taste a little honey... and, lo, I must die' (1 Samuel 14:27, 43)?","Did you know the people overruled Saul's death sentence on Jonathan, declaring 'there shall not one hair of his head fall to the ground' (1 Samuel 14:45)?"], questions:[
{q:"Whom did Jonathan take with him on his secret raid?", options:["His armor-bearer","His brother Ishui","Abner the captain","A company of fifty"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 14:1",verse:"Now it came to pass upon a day, that Jonathan the son of Saul said unto the young man that bare his armour, Come, and let us go over to the Philistines’ garrison, that is on the other side. But he told not his father.", insight:"One loyal companion; God often starts His greatest works with two faithful hearts."},
{q:"Where was Saul tarrying while Jonathan slipped away?", options:["Under a pomegranate tree in Migron","In the caves of Adullam","At the altar in Gilgal","On the walls of Gibeah"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 14:2",verse:"And Saul tarried in the uttermost part of Gibeah under a pomegranate tree which is in Migron: and the people that were with him were about six hundred men;", insight:"Under a pomegranate tree, waiting; passivity in the king made room for courage in the son."},
{q:"Who was the priest wearing the ephod with Saul?", options:["Ahiah the son of Ahitub","Abiathar the son of Ahimelech","Zadok the priest","Eli of Shiloh"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 14:3",verse:"And Ahiah, the son of Ahitub, I-chabod’s brother, the son of Phinehas, the son of Eli, the LORD’S priest in Shiloh, wearing an ephod. And the people knew not that Jonathan was gone.", insight:"Ahiah wore the ephod; the forms of worship were present, but the faith was elsewhere."},
{q:"What were the names of the sharp rocks by the pass?", options:["Bozez and Seneh","Mizpeh and Ramah","Ebal and Gerizim","Tabor and Hermon"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 14:4",verse:"And between the passages, by which Jonathan sought to go over unto the Philistines’ garrison, there was a sharp rock on the one side and a sharp rock on the other side: and the name of the one was Bozez, and the name of the other Seneh.", insight:"Bozez and Seneh, thorn and tooth; even the landscape warned how steep the climb was."},
{q:"What did Jonathan say about the LORD saving?", options:["There is no restraint to the LORD to save by many or by few","The LORD saves only the righteous","The LORD needs a great army","The LORD has forsaken Israel"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 14:6",verse:"And Jonathan said to the young man that bare his armour, Come, and let us go over unto the garrison of these uncircumcised: it may be that the LORD will work for us: for there is no restraint to the LORD to save by many or by few.", insight:"No restraint; the size of the army never limits the size of the God."},
{q:"What did Jonathan's armor-bearer answer him?", options:["Do all that is in thine heart; I am with thee","Turn back, this is madness","Let me go alone instead","Wait for Saul's command"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 14:7",verse:"And his armourbearer said unto him, Do all that is in thine heart: turn thee; behold, I am with thee according to thy heart.", insight:"'I am with thee'; loyalty like that is a gift from God."},
{q:"What sign did Jonathan set for the attack?", options:["If they say, Come up to us, then the LORD has delivered them","If the sun darkens at noon","If a trumpet sounds from the camp","If the rocks fall from the cliff"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 14:10",verse:"But if they say thus, Come up unto us; then we will go up: for the LORD hath delivered them into our hand: and this shall be a sign unto us.", insight:"A simple sign, boldly tested; faith asks God to confirm, then acts."},
{q:"What did the Philistines say when the two appeared?", options:["Behold, the Hebrews come forth out of the holes where they hid","Surrender now and we will spare you","Who are these two fools","Sound the alarm in the camp"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 14:11",verse:"And both of them discovered themselves unto the garrison of the Philistines: and the Philistines said, Behold, the Hebrews come forth out of the holes where they had hid themselves.", insight:"They mocked the two; scorn is often the last sound before the fall."},
{q:"How many did Jonathan and his armor-bearer kill in the first slaughter?", options:["About twenty men, within half an acre","About two hundred men","About six hundred men","Only the captain of the garrison"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 14:14",verse:"And that first slaughter, which Jonathan and his armourbearer made, was about twenty men, within as it were an half acre of land, which a yoke of oxen might plow.", insight:"Twenty men in half an acre; two faithful men, and God did the multiplying."},
{q:"What spread through the Philistine host after the slaughter?", options:["Trembling, and the earth quaked","A great shout of triumph","A sudden darkness","A plague of boils"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 14:15",verse:"And there was trembling in the host, in the field, and among all the people: the garrison, and the spoilers, they also trembled, and the earth quaked: so it was a very great trembling.", insight:"The earth itself quaked; when God fights, creation trembles with Him."},
{q:"What did Saul's watchmen see from Gibeah?", options:["The multitude melting away, beating down one another","A cloud of dust from the east","Jonathan returning with spoil","The Philistines marching in order"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 14:16",verse:"And the watchmen of Saul in Gibeah of Benjamin looked; and, behold, the multitude melted away, and they went on beating down one another.", insight:"The enemy melting; God's victories often begin where no Israelite sword has swung."},
{q:"Who was missing when Saul numbered the people?", options:["Jonathan and his armor-bearer","Abner and his captains","Ahiah the priest","Sixty men of Benjamin"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 14:17",verse:"Then said Saul unto the people that were with him, Number now, and see who is gone from us. And when they had numbered, behold, Jonathan and his armourbearer were not there.", insight:"Two men gone, and the whole battle changed; never underestimate the absent faithful."},
{q:"What did Saul tell Ahiah to bring?", options:["The ark of God","The ephod of judgment","The tables of the covenant","The golden calf"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 14:18",verse:"And Saul said unto Ahiah, Bring hither the ark of God. For the ark of God was at that time with the children of Israel.", insight:"He reached for the ark in the crisis; religion used as a tool is not the same as faith."},
{q:"Why did Saul tell the priest, Withdraw thine hand?", options:["Because the noise in the Philistines' host kept increasing","Because the ark was too heavy","Because Samuel had arrived","Because the sun was setting"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 14:19",verse:"And it came to pass, while Saul talked unto the priest, that the noise that was in the host of the Philistines went on and increased: and Saul said unto the priest, Withdraw thine hand.", insight:"He stopped seeking God mid-prayer; impatience interrupts the very help it wants."},
{q:"What oath did Saul lay upon the people?", options:["Cursed be the man that eats any food until evening","Cursed be the man that flees from battle","Cursed be the man that touches the spoil","Cursed be the man that speaks to Jonathan"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 14:24",verse:"And the men of Israel were distressed that day: for Saul had adjured the people, saying, Cursed be the man that eateth any food until evening, that I may be avenged on mine enemies. So none of the people tasted any food.", insight:"A rash vow in the heat of war; leaders should feed their people, not starve them for revenge."},
{q:"What did Jonathan eat, not knowing of his father's oath?", options:["Honey, dipping the end of his rod in the honeycomb","Bread from the camp","Figs from a tree","Water from the brook"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 14:27",verse:"But Jonathan heard not when his father charged the people with the oath: wherefore he put forth the end of the rod that was in his hand, and dipped it in an honeycomb, and put his hand to his mouth; and his eyes were enlightened.", insight:"A taste of honey in innocence; not every broken rule is a sin of the heart."},
{q:"What did the honey do for Jonathan?", options:["His eyes were enlightened","He fell into a deep sleep","He became faint and weak","He was filled with anger"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 14:27",verse:"But Jonathan heard not when his father charged the people with the oath: wherefore he put forth the end of the rod that was in his hand, and dipped it in an honeycomb, and put his hand to his mouth; and his eyes were enlightened.", insight:"His eyes were enlightened; God's good gifts restore the weary."},
{q:"What did the famished people do that evening?", options:["Flew upon the spoil and ate the meat with the blood","Refused to eat until morning","Shared their food with Jonathan","Offered the spoil to the LORD"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 14:32",verse:"And the people flew upon the spoil, and took sheep, and oxen, and calves, and slew them on the ground: and the people did eat them with the blood.", insight:"They ate with the blood; Saul's harsh oath drove hungry men into sin."},
{q:"How was Jonathan's guilt discovered?", options:["By casting lots, and Jonathan was taken","By a witness who saw him","By Samuel's prophecy","By Saul's dream"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 14:42",verse:"And Saul said, Cast lots between me and Jonathan my son. And Jonathan was taken.", insight:"The lot found him out; hidden things come to light, even innocent ones."},
{q:"How did the people rescue Jonathan from Saul's sentence?", options:["They declared not one hair of his head should fall to the ground","They hid him in the caves","They bribed the guards","They crowned him king instead"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 14:45",verse:"And the people said unto Saul, Shall Jonathan die, who hath wrought this great salvation in Israel? God forbid: as the LORD liveth, there shall not one hair of his head fall to the ground; for he hath wrought with God this day. So the people rescued Jonathan, that he died not.", insight:"The people stood between the king and the innocent; sometimes the crowd is righter than the crown."}
]},
{n:15, name:"Saul Rejected as King", seal:{ref:"1 Samuel 15:22",verse:"And Samuel said, Hath the LORD as great delight in burnt offerings and sacrifices, as in obeying the voice of the LORD? Behold, to obey is better than sacrifice, and to hearken than the fat of rams."}, facts:["Did you know Saul spared King Agag and the best livestock, though God's command was 'utterly destroy all that they have, and spare them not' (1 Samuel 15:3, 9)?","Did you know Samuel's answer to Saul's sacrifice excuse became one of the most quoted lines in Scripture: 'to obey is better than sacrifice' (1 Samuel 15:22)?","Did you know Saul tore Samuel's mantle as he turned to leave, and Samuel read it as a sign: 'The LORD hath rent the kingdom of Israel from thee this day' (1 Samuel 15:27-28)?"], questions:[
{q:"What did Samuel remind Saul at the start?", options:["The LORD sent me to anoint thee king","The people chose thee by lot","Thou wast the tallest in Israel","Thy father Kish sent thee"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 15:1",verse:"Samuel also said unto Saul, The LORD sent me to anoint thee to be king over his people, over Israel: now therefore hearken thou unto the voice of the words of the LORD.", insight:"Anointed by God through Samuel; the mission came with the memory of the mercy."},
{q:"What had Amalek done that the LORD remembered?", options:["Laid wait for Israel in the way when they came up from Egypt","Stolen the ark of the covenant","Killed the priests at Nob","Besieged Jabesh-gilead"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 15:2",verse:"Thus saith the LORD of hosts, I remember that which Amalek did to Israel, how he laid wait for him in the way, when he came up from Egypt.", insight:"God remembered an old ambush; His justice keeps perfect books."},
{q:"How complete was the destruction of Amalek to be?", options:["Utterly destroy all, sparing neither man nor beast","Spare the women and children","Take the livestock as spoil","Destroy the cities but spare the king"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 15:3",verse:"Now go and smite Amalek, and utterly destroy all that they have, and spare them not; but slay both man and woman, infant and suckling, ox and sheep, camel and ass.", insight:"Utterly destroy; half obedience was the very thing Saul would try."},
{q:"How many did Saul number at Telaim?", options:["Two hundred thousand footmen and ten thousand men of Judah","Three hundred thousand of Israel","Six hundred chosen men","Thirty thousand chariots"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 15:4",verse:"And Saul gathered the people together, and numbered them in Telaim, two hundred thousand footmen, and ten thousand men of Judah.", insight:"Two hundred and ten thousand; a great army for a simple command: obey."},
{q:"What warning did Saul give the Kenites?", options:["Depart from among the Amalekites, lest I destroy you with them","Flee to Egypt before nightfall","Hide your livestock in the caves","Join my army or perish"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 15:6",verse:"And Saul said unto the Kenites, Go, depart, get you down from among the Amalekites, lest I destroy you with them: for ye shewed kindness to all the children of Israel, when they came up out of Egypt. So the Kenites departed from among the Amalekites.", insight:"He warned the innocent to leave; even in judgment, Saul knew how to show mercy."},
{q:"Why did the Kenites deserve Saul's warning?", options:["They had shown kindness to Israel coming up from Egypt","They had paid tribute to Saul","They were kin to Saul's wife","They had fought against Amalek"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 15:6",verse:"And Saul said unto the Kenites, Go, depart, get you down from among the Amalekites, lest I destroy you with them: for ye shewed kindness to all the children of Israel, when they came up out of Egypt. So the Kenites departed from among the Amalekites.", insight:"Kindness remembered across generations; God does not forget old mercies."},
{q:"How far did Saul smite the Amalekites?", options:["From Havilah to Shur, over against Egypt","From Dan to Beersheba","From the Jordan to the sea","From Gilgal to Carmel"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 15:7",verse:"And Saul smote the Amalekites from Havilah until thou comest to Shur, that is over against Egypt.", insight:"From Havilah to Shur; the campaign was thorough, the obedience was not."},
{q:"What did Saul do with Agag the king of Amalek?", options:["Took him alive","Slew him at once","Sent him to Samuel","Made him a servant"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 15:8",verse:"And he took Agag the king of the Amalekites alive, and utterly destroyed all the people with the edge of the sword.", insight:"Alive, as a trophy; disobedience often keeps a souvenir."},
{q:"What did Saul and the people spare?", options:["The best of the sheep, oxen, fatlings, and lambs","Only the camels and asses","The gold and silver vessels","The women and children"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 15:9",verse:"But Saul and the people spared Agag, and the best of the sheep, and of the oxen, and of the fatlings, and the lambs, and all that was good, and would not utterly destroy them: but every thing that was vile and refuse, that they destroyed utterly.", insight:"The best of the flock; they called it prudence, God called it rebellion."},
{q:"What word of the LORD came to Samuel?", options:["It repenteth Me that I have set up Saul to be king","I will establish Saul's house forever","Send for David the son of Jesse","The kingdom shall pass to Jonathan"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 15:10",verse:"Then came the word of the LORD unto Samuel, saying,", insight:"'It repenteth Me'; when God grieves over a king, the throne is already shaking."},
{q:"What did Samuel do all that night?", options:["Cried unto the LORD","Slept in sorrow","Wrote a lament","Fled to Ramah"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 15:11",verse:"It repenteth me that I have set up Saul to be king: for he is turned back from following me, and hath not performed my commandments. And it grieved Samuel; and he cried unto the LORD all night.", insight:"He cried all night; a prophet's grief is the echo of God's."},
{q:"Where had Saul set up a monument to himself?", options:["At Carmel","At Gilgal","At Bethel","At Mizpeh"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 15:12",verse:"And when Samuel rose early to meet Saul in the morning, it was told Samuel, saying, Saul came to Carmel, and, behold, he set him up a place, and is gone about, and passed on, and gone down to Gilgal.", insight:"A monument at Carmel; self-glory was already replacing obedience."},
{q:"What did Saul claim when Samuel met him?", options:["I have performed the commandment of the LORD","I have spared the best for sacrifice","The people forced my hand","Agag deserved to live"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 15:13",verse:"And Samuel came to Saul: and Saul said unto him, Blessed be thou of the LORD: I have performed the commandment of the LORD.", insight:"'I have performed'; the first lie of disobedience is to call it obedience."},
{q:"What did Samuel say he could hear?", options:["The bleating of the sheep and the lowing of the oxen","The weeping of the captives","The trumpets of the army","The grinding of the mills"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 15:14",verse:"And Samuel said, What meaneth then this bleating of the sheep in mine ears, and the lowing of the oxen which I hear?", insight:"The sheep gave him away; hidden compromise always makes a noise."},
{q:"What excuse did Saul offer for the spared livestock?", options:["The people spared the best to sacrifice to the LORD","They were needed for the army","The Kenites had claimed them","Samuel had allowed it"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 15:15",verse:"And Saul said, They have brought them from the Amalekites: for the people spared the best of the sheep and of the oxen, to sacrifice unto the LORD thy God; and the rest we have utterly destroyed.", insight:"Blame the people, flatter God; excuses multiply where repentance is due."},
{q:"What did Samuel say is better than sacrifice?", options:["To obey, and to hearken than the fat of rams","To pray and to fast","To give to the poor","To build altars"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 15:22",verse:"And Samuel said, Hath the LORD as great delight in burnt offerings and sacrifices, as in obeying the voice of the LORD? Behold, to obey is better than sacrifice, and to hearken than the fat of rams.", insight:"Obey is better than sacrifice; God wants the heart before the offering."},
{q:"What is rebellion like, according to Samuel?", options:["As the sin of witchcraft, and stubbornness as idolatry","As a small and forgivable fault","As the error of the ignorant","As the weakness of the flesh"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 15:23",verse:"For rebellion is as the sin of witchcraft, and stubbornness is as iniquity and idolatry. Because thou hast rejected the word of the LORD, he hath also rejected thee from being king.", insight:"Rebellion is witchcraft; God names our polished sins with ugly, honest words."},
{q:"Why did Saul admit he had transgressed?", options:["Because he feared the people and obeyed their voice","Because Samuel threatened him","Because the army mutinied","Because Agag escaped"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 15:24",verse:"And Saul said unto Samuel, I have sinned: for I have transgressed the commandment of the LORD, and thy words: because I feared the people, and obeyed their voice.", insight:"He feared the people more than God; the fear of man is a snare for kings too."},
{q:"What happened when Saul seized Samuel's mantle?", options:["He laid hold on the skirt, and it tore","Samuel struck his hand away","The mantle caught fire","Nothing happened at all"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 15:27",verse:"And as Samuel turned about to go away, he laid hold upon the skirt of his mantle, and it rent.", insight:"The torn skirt preached the sermon; kingdoms, like cloth, tear along the lines of disobedience."},
{q:"What did Samuel do to Agag at Gilgal?", options:["Hewed him in pieces before the LORD","Set him free with a warning","Sent him into exile","Made him a prisoner of war"], a:0, ref:"1 Samuel 15:33",verse:"And Samuel said, As thy sword hath made women childless, so shall thy mother be childless among women. And Samuel hewed Agag in pieces before the LORD in Gilgal.", insight:"What Saul spared, Samuel finished; God's unfinished judgments do not stay unfinished."}
]}
]);
